nd before the noun ('my father'), in Arabic the pronoun attaches directly to the **END** of the noun as a suffix:\n- **-ِي** (-ī [EE]) -> **My** (e.g. *abī* [ah-BEE] = my father, *ummī* [oom-MEE] = my mother, *akhī* = my brother, *ukhtī* = my sister)\n- **-كَ** (-ka [KAH]) -> **Your (masculine singular)** (e.g. *ismuka* = your name, *akhūka* = your brother)\n- **-كِ** (-ki [KEE]) -> **Your (feminine singular)** (e.g. *ismuki* = your name, *akhūki* = your brother)\n- **-هُ** (-hu [HOO]) -> **His** (e.g. *abūhu* = his father, *ukhtuhu* = his sister)\n- **-هَا** (-hā [HAH]) -> **Her** (e.g. *abūhā* = her father, *usratuhā* = her family)\n- **-نَا** (-nā [NAH]) -> **Our** (e.g. *baytunā* = our house, *usratunā* = our family)\n\n*Note on Ta Marbūṭa (ة):* When attaching a suffix to a feminine word ending in ة, the ة changes into an open pronounced **ت** (t): e.g. *usrah* (أُسْرَة) + *-ī* -> *usratī* (أُسْرَتِي = my family).",
