سَاعِدُنَا yusāʿidunā - he helps us)\n- Them (Plural): **-هُمْ (-hum)** (أُحِبُّهُمْ uḥibbuhum - I like them)\n\nKey Service Expression: 'هَلْ يُمْكِنُكَ أَنْ تُسَاعِدَنِي؟' (Hal yumkinuka an tusāʿidanī? - Can you help me?).",
+            "explanation": "In Arabic, direct object pronouns (me, you, him, her, us, them) are NOT separate words! Instead, they attach directly to the end of the conjugated verb as **Attached Pronoun Suffixes** (*aḍ-Ḍamā'ir al-Muttaṣilah*):\n\nDirect Object Suffix Paradigm with Verbs:\n- Me (I): **-نِي (-nī)** (e.g. يُسَاعِدُنِي yusāʿidunī - he helps me; يُعْجِبُنِي yuʿjibunī - it pleases me / I like it)\n- You (m.): **-كَ (-ka)** (يُسَاعِدُكَ yusāʿiduka - he helps you m.)\n- You (f.): **-كِ (-ki)** (يُسَاعِدُكِ yusāʿiduki - he helps you f.)\n- Him / It (m.): **-هُ (-hu)** (أَعْرِفُهُ aʿrifuhu - I know him / I know it)\n- Her / It (f.): **-هَا (-hā)** (أَطْلُبُهَا aṭlubuhā - I order it f. [e.g. as-salaṭah])\n- Us (We): **-نَا (-nā)** (يُسَاعِدُنَا yusāʿidunā - he helps us)\n- Them (Plural): **-هُمْ (-hum)** (أُحِبُّهُمْ uḥibbuhum - I like them)\n\nKey Service Expression: 'هَلْ يُمْكِنُكَ أَنْ تُسَاعِدَنِي؟' (Hal yumkinuka an tusāʿidanī? - Can you help me?).",
