nceived 600 years before René Descartes's *Cogito Ergo Sum*;\n    - Imagine a human created instantaneously suspended in empty air with no sight, deafened, with limbs spread wide, experiencing zero tactile sensory input;\n    - Ibn Sina proves that despite zero sensory contact with the physical world, the flying man would still possess direct, indubitable consciousness of his own soul's existence (*\"I am\"*);\n  - **The Proof of the Sincere (برهان الصديقين / Proof of the Necessary Existent)**:\n    - Divided all reality into: **Contingent Beings (ممكن الوجود)** whose existence requires an external cause, and the **Necessary Existent (واجب الوجود)** whose essence is existence itself, requiring no cause—God.",
