sed on the real historical discovery of 40 royal mummies of ancient Egyptian Pharaohs (including Ramesses II and Seti I) hidden in the cliffs of Luxor, secretly looted for generations by the Horabat mountain clan;\n  - **The Poetic Moral Awakening**:\n    - Young protagonist Wannis discovers his family survives by desecrating and selling the mummies of their own royal ancestors, confronting the moral choice between tribal loyalty and national historical consciousness;\n  - **Visual & Acoustic Sovereignty**:\n    - Monumental slow tracking shots across desert limestone cliffs, classical Arabic dialogue spoken like sacred poetry, profound meditative silence, and an avant-garde score by Italian composer Mario Nascimbene.",
